  "content": {
    "id": "p7-p4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $p=\\dfrac{\\log(a)}{\\log(x)}$. Show that $x^p=a$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^p=a$\n\nChange base to write $p=\\log_x(a)$, then apply the inverse identity.",
      "worked": "The given quotient is defined for $a>0$, $x>0$ and $x\\ne1$. By change of base, $p=\\log_x(a)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^p&=x^{\\log_x(a)}\\\\x^p&=a.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 16
